AB  - In this work, a hybrid deep learning framework based on a combination of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and bidirectional long short-term memory networks (BiLSTMs) was employed for the multiclass classification of pathogenic single-nucleotide variations (SNVs) and small insertions/deletions across twenty different monogenic disorders. Each mutation was represented as a 101-base-pair window, allowing for the preservation of the biologically relevant flanking sequence while keeping the problem computationally feasible. To account for the class imbalance, a set of augmentation strategies was employed. The framework utilizes a combination of multiscale convolutional 1D filters for motif extraction and bidirectional long short-term memory networks for the extraction of long-range contextual dependencies. This combination allows for robust classification across the heterogeneous set of disease classes. With the use of class-weighted loss and stratified cross-validation, the framework achieved an overall accuracy of 94.7 %, a mean class-weighted F1 score of 0.93, and a mean AUC-PR score of 0.98. Per-class ROC-AUC values approach 1.00. Rapid convergence was observed over the course of ∼12 epochs, and strong class-level separability was achieved. To further validate the framework’s potential for use in genomic interpretation, motif-level interpretability results highlight the importance of certain biologically meaningful patterns. Although the dataset composition was a controlled environment, the results demonstrate the potential for the use of the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM framework as a scalable and high-throughput solution for the classification of monogenic disease variants.
