AB  - Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) affects approximately 1 in 36 children globally, necessitating early diagnosis to enable timely interventions that significantly improve developmental outcomes. Current diagnostic protocols rely on time-intensive behavioral assessments requiring specialized expertise, resulting in substantial diagnostic delays. This study presents a hybrid deep learning framework integrating Convolutional Neural Networks (ResNet-152 with CBAM) and Vision Transformers (ViT-Large) through bidirectional cross-attention fusion for automated ASD classification from facial images. We systematically evaluate three complementary architectures (CNN-based, Transformer-based, and hybrid) along with a weighted ensemble approach enhanced by Test-Time Augmentation. Our ensemble model outperforms the previous best results by 0.67 percentage points (91.33% in Nature Scientific Reports) achieving results in accuracy (92.00% ± 0.42%), precision (93.15% ± 0.38%), recall (90.67% ± 0.51%), F1-score (0.9189 ± 0.0041) and AUC (0.9674 ± 0.0028) with mean ± standard deviation across 5-fold cross-validation. Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping reveals model focus on clinically relevant periocular regions, providing interpretable predictions aligned with established clinical observations. Ablation studies confirm Test-Time Augmentation contributes 2.34 percentage points accuracy improvement, while weighted ensemble optimization adds 1.33 percentage points over simple averaging. The cross-attention fusion mechanism enables dynamic information exchange between CNN and Transformer feature spaces, synergistically combining local spatial pattern recognition with global contextual understanding. Results establish that hybrid architectures with sophisticated fusion mechanisms outperform single-paradigm approaches for ASD classification. This research demonstrates AI-assisted screening tool potential to democratize early ASD identification, reduce diagnostic delays, and facilitate timely intervention, ultimately improving outcomes for children with ASD worldwide.
