KW  - Ground-penetrating radar (GPR)
KW  - Deep-learning (DL)
KW  - U-Net
KW  - 3D
KW  - Interpolation
KW  - Reconstruction
AB  - Three-dimensional (3D) ground-penetrating radar (GPR) surveys have gained significant attention for their ability to capture detailed subsurface structures. However, acquiring data along a series of densely spaced parallel survey lines is often impractical due to long acquisition times. As a result, many 3D GPR surveys involve the collection of high-density measurements along individual survey lines but maintain significantly wider gaps between the lines, creating a pronounced resolution bias in the across-line direction. To address this issue, current methods often rely on basic interpolation techniques such as linear interpolation and kriging. However, these approaches tend to produce overly smooth and unrealistic results, failing to accurately preserve intricate spatial patterns. This study introduces a deep-learning-based approach utilizing a U-Net architecture, specifically designed to enhance the density of sparse 3D GPR data in the across-line direction. Our method applies sequential 2D simulations along and across survey lines, leveraging existing GPR profiles as training data to retain fine spatial details. To enrich the training dataset, we incorporate pseudo-GPR data synthesized from natural images alongside field GPR measurements. We validate our U-Net-based approach on both a synthetic and a field GPR dataset, achieving superior reconstruction accuracy.
