AB  - Smoothed particle hydrodynamics (SPH) is a Lagrangian, particle-based method that has demonstrated effectiveness in the analysis of free-surface flows. However, SPH requires neighbour searches to compute particle interactions using kernel functions, and this procedure constitutes a major computational bottleneck. This study introduces a deep learning model that predicts free-surface flows within a Lagrangian forms without relying on neighbour searches, enabling significant computational acceleration. However, a drawback of this approach is that by neglecting particle–particle interactions, numerical errors accumulate owing to particle clustering. To address this issue, we introduce a hybrid strategy that alternates between the deep learning model and conventional SPH updates. By combining the efficiency of the learning-based model with the accuracy of SPH, our method achieved both high speed and high fidelity in free-surface flow prediction. The proposed method is comprehensively and neutrally validated using the WaterDrop problem, which is a standard benchmark for free-surface flows. Under identical programming languages and computational environments, comparisons with both explicit and implicit schemes demonstrated that the proposed approach achieves an acceleration of up to approximately four times.
